Configure and Manage sudo
===============================================================
Goal: Give a junior admin 'jradmin' limited sudo rights (restart
httpd and read httpd logs only), let DBA 'dba1' run any command as
user 'mysql' without a password, log all sudo usage to
/var/log/sudo.log, then test and check the log.


STEP 1: Create the users
------------------------
$ sudo useradd jradmin
$ sudo passwd jradmin
(type the new password twice)

$ sudo useradd dba1
$ sudo passwd dba1
(type the new password twice)

Verify:
$ id jradmin
$ id dba1


STEP 2: Create the sudoers rule for jradmin
-------------------------------------------
Use visudo for the drop-in file (it checks the syntax for you):
$ sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/jradmin

(Your lab book used: sudo nano /etc/sudoers.d/jradmin. That also
 works, but a typo can break sudo. visudo -f is safer.)

Type these 3 lines:

Cmnd_Alias HTTPD_RESTART = /usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd
Cmnd_Alias HTTPD_LOGS = /usr/bin/tail /var/log/httpd/*
jradmin ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: HTTPD_RESTART, HTTPD_LOGS

Save and exit:
  nano -> Ctrl+O, Enter, Ctrl+X
  vi   -> Esc, then :wq

Set the correct permissions on the file:
$ sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/jradmin

Check syntax of all sudoers files:
$ sudo visudo -c


STEP 3: Create the sudoers rule for dba1
----------------------------------------
Task: dba1 can run ANY command as user 'mysql' without a password,
in its own file /etc/sudoers.d/dba1.

$ sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/dba1

Type this line:

dba1 ALL=(mysql) NOPASSWD: ALL

Save and exit, then:
$ sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/dba1
$ sudo visudo -c

(Your lab book put "dba1 ALL=(root) NOPASSWD:LOCATE" into the main
 sudoers file with visudo, allowing only updatedb as root. That
 does not match the task. The line above is what the task asks.)


STEP 4: Enable sudo logging to /var/log/sudo.log
------------------------------------------------
$ sudo visudo

Add this line (for example under the other "Defaults" lines):

Defaults logfile="/var/log/sudo.log"

Save and exit (vi: Esc, then :wq).

Or put it in its own drop-in file:
$ sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/logging
  Defaults logfile="/var/log/sudo.log"
$ sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/logging


STEP 5: Validate and test
-------------------------
Make sure httpd exists, otherwise the restart test fails with
"Unit httpd.service not found" (as in your lab book):

$ sudo dnf install httpd -y
$ sudo systemctl enable --now httpd

Test jradmin, ALLOWED commands:
$ su - jradmin
$ sudo systemctl restart httpd
$ sudo tail /var/log/httpd/error_log
(these should work without errors)

Test jradmin, an UNAUTHORISED command (should be denied):
$ sudo reboot
$ sudo cat /var/log/sudo.log
(expected: "Sorry, user jradmin is not allowed to execute ...")

Leave jradmin:
$ exit

Test dba1 (optional, needs a 'mysql' user on the system):
$ sudo dnf install mariadb-server -y     (creates the mysql user)
$ su - dba1
$ sudo -u mysql whoami
(should print: mysql, with no password prompt)
$ exit


STEP 6: Check the sudo log
--------------------------
$ sudo tail -f /var/log/sudo.log
(Ctrl+C to stop)

Or just view it once:
$ sudo cat /var/log/sudo.log

You should see lines like:
  jradmin : TTY=pts/0 ; PWD=/home/jradmin ; USER=root ;
            COMMAND=/usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd
  jradmin : command not allowed ; TTY=pts/0 ; PWD=/home/jradmin ;
            USER=root ; COMMAND=/bin/cat /var/log/sudo.log

"command not allowed" proves the unauthorised attempt was logged.


QUICK COMMAND SUMMARY
---------------------
sudo useradd jradmin ; sudo passwd jradmin        -> create user
sudo useradd dba1 ; sudo passwd dba1              -> create user
sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/jradmin             -> jradmin rules
sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/jradmin             -> permissions
sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/dba1                -> dba1 rule
sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/dba1                -> permissions
sudo visudo                                       -> add Defaults logfile
sudo visudo -c                                    -> check syntax
su - jradmin                                      -> switch user to test
sudo systemctl restart httpd                      -> allowed test
sudo reboot                                       -> denied test
sudo tail -f /var/log/sudo.log                    -> verify log
